  "hints": [
    "Base case: for n = 0, 5⁰ − 1 = 0, and 24 divides 0.",
    "5^(2(k + 1)) = 25·5^(2k). Rewrite 25·5^(2k) − 1 so that the block 5^(2k) − 1 appears.",
    "25·5^(2k) − 1 = 25(5^(2k) − 1) + 24 = 25·24m + 24. Factor out 24."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Base case: for $n = 0$, $5^{0} - 1 = 0 = 24 \\cdot 0$, so $24 \\mid (5^{0} - 1)$.",
    "Inductive step: suppose $24 \\mid (5^{2k} - 1)$ for some $k \\ge 0$, so $5^{2k} - 1 = 24m$ for an integer $m$.",
    "Then $5^{2(k+1)} - 1 = 25 \\cdot 5^{2k} - 1 = 25(5^{2k} - 1) + 24 = 25 \\cdot 24m + 24 = 24(25m + 1)$, and $25m + 1$ is an integer, so $24 \\mid (5^{2(k+1)} - 1)$.",
    "By induction, $24 \\mid (5^{2n} - 1)$ for every integer $n \\ge 0$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
